RUNWAY NOTES
Thursday, March 5, 2026

Usage pricing without pricing yourself out

A founder asked me last week whether to move from seats to usage. The honest answer is that the question is wrong. Nobody moves from seats to usage; they move from one bundle of predictability and upside to a different one, and the work is in choosing the bundle.

Pure usage is what your finance team fears: revenue that goes down when the customer has a quiet month, a forecast that is a guess, and a renewal conversation in which the customer has a spreadsheet of exactly what they used and exactly what it cost you. It is also what your customers' procurement teams increasingly demand, because it looks like risk transfer.

The compromise that works, in the companies I have watched do it well, has three parts. A platform fee that covers your fixed cost of serving the account and is not negotiable. A committed usage tier, bought annually, priced at a discount to the rack rate. And overage priced at rack, billed monthly, with a mechanism to true up the commitment mid-year if the customer is consistently over.

The committed tier does most of the work. It gives finance an ARR number they can defend, it gives the customer a budget number they can defend, and it converts the awkward "you used more than you paid for" conversation into "you are growing, let's raise the commitment," which is a conversation salespeople like having.

One warning. If your cost of goods scales with usage (compute, tokens, third-party data), price the overage with a margin that survives the customer's worst month, not their average one. I have seen two companies this year with negative gross margin on their largest account because the overage was priced off a model cost that has since doubled.

Benchmarks: among the Series A and B companies in our set that report a hybrid model, the platform fee is 20-35% of the average contract, the committed tier covers 70-85% of realized usage, and overage runs 10-25% above the committed rate.

